	abstract = {Tritium-powered betavoltaic micropower sources using contact potential difference ({CPD)} are demonstrated. Thermally stable scandium tritide thin films with a surface activity of 15{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}{mCi/cm2} were used as the beta particle source. The electrical field created by the work function difference between the {ScT} film and a platinum or copper electrode was used to separate the beta-generated electrical charge carriers. Open circuit voltages of 0.5 and 0.16{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}V and short circuit current densities of 2.7 and 5.3{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}{nA/cm2} were achieved for gaseous and solid dielectric media-based {CPD} cells, respectively.},
