	abstract = {We report the fabrication of waveguides in lithium tantalate using a 250{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}{kHz} high-repetition rate ultrafast laser at 771{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}nm and the characterization of the resulting laser induced structure with second harmonic microscopy. Waveguides operating at the 1.5{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}{\^I}{\OE}m telecommunication wavelength were formed above and below the focal volume using pulse energies ranging from 100{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}to{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}1.6{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}J and translation speeds from 100{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}{\^I}{\OE}m/s{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}to{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}5{\^a}{\^A}{\^A}mm/s. The second harmonic microscopy reveals no degradation of the electro-optic coefficient in the guiding region above the focal volume.},
