	abstract = {Currently little data exists on the fracture and sub-critical crack growth behavior of single crystal silicon, a common material for micromachined devices. In order to obtain stable crack growth in silicon, we utilize a decreasing stress intensity specimen geometry. We present new data on crack growth rates in silicon as a function of applied stress intensity (K). We made direct measurements of these values via optical and electrical resistance techniques under monotonic loading conditions. We also present new data on the critical stress intensity (K{sub Ic}) of silicon for a sample of micromachined proportions. (6 Refs.)},
